{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In successful edits (train.parquet), the variability of synthetic accessibility changes (SA_delta) is greater for replace operations than for insert or delete operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify and compare the variability (standard deviation) of SA_delta across action types in successful edits and test for unequal variances.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null SA_delta.\n2. Group by action_type and compute the sample standard deviation of SA_delta for each group.\n3. Bootstrap the standard deviations (1,000 resamples) within each action_type to derive 95% confidence intervals for each group’s SD.\n4. Perform an overall Levene’s test for equality of variances across the three action types.\n5. If Levene’s test is significant, perform pairwise variance tests (e.g., pairwise Levene or Fligner–Killeen) with Bonferroni correction.\n6. Visualize boxplots of SA_delta by action_type, overlaying error bars or violin widths to illustrate variance differences.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of SD and 95% CIs of SA_delta for replace, insert, delete.\n2. Levene’s test statistic and p-value.\n3. Pairwise variance test results with adjusted p-values.\n4. Boxplot or violin plot of SA_delta distributions by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"For the water solubility optimization task (task_id=101) in successful edits, breaking the Murcko scaffold results in higher target_delta than retaining the scaffold.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare target_delta distributions between scaffold-retained and scaffold-broken groups for task_id=101 and test for significant differences.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to task_id=101, dropping rows with null target_delta or murcko_scaffold_retained.\n2. Split the data into two groups based on murcko_scaffold_retained (true/false).\n3. Compute group-wise mean, median, and bootstrapped (1,000 resamples) 95% CIs for target_delta.\n4. Test each group for normality (e.g., Shapiro–Wilk). Based on results, choose either Welch’s t-test or Mann–Whitney U test to compare group central tendencies.\n5. Perform the chosen test and record statistic and p-value.\n6. Visualize the two distributions using side-by-side boxplots or violin plots, annotating means or medians with their CIs.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table of mean, median, and 95% CIs of target_delta for scaffold-retained vs broken (task 101).\n2. Normality test results for each group.\n3. Test statistic and p-value for the group comparison.\n4. Boxplot or violin plot comparing the two distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer natural language prompts are associated with lower editing success rates across all tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the relationship between prompt length and the probability of a successful edit by combining train.parquet and train_neg.parquet records.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both train.parquet (label success=1) and train_neg.parquet (label success=0), drop rows with null prompt.\n2. Create a new column prompt_length (e.g., count of words or characters in the prompt).\n3. Combine the two datasets into one and compute binned success rates by prompt_length decile.\n4. Fit a logistic regression model: success ~ prompt_length + task_id (as categorical) + action_type.\n5. Extract the coefficient, standard error, and p-value for prompt_length.\n6. Plot observed success rate vs prompt_length bins and overlay the fitted logistic curve.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Combined dataset with new prompt_length column.\n2. Table of prompt_length bins vs observed success rates.\n3. Logistic regression summary showing coefficient for prompt_length.\n4. Plot of success probability vs prompt_length with model fit."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"In unsuccessful edits (train_neg.parquet), larger MW_delta is associated with more negative QED_delta after adjusting for SA_delta and action_type.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether MW_delta negatively predicts QED_delta in failed edits while controlling for SA_delta and action_type.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train_neg.parquet and drop rows with null MW_delta, QED_delta, or SA_delta.\n2. Encode action_type as categorical dummies.\n3. Fit a linear regression: QED_delta ~ MW_delta + SA_delta + action_type.\n4. Report the coefficient for MW_delta, its standard error, t-statistic, and p-value.\n5. Generate diagnostic plots: residuals vs fitted, Q–Q plot of residuals.\n6. Optionally compute the partial correlation between MW_delta and QED_delta controlling for SA_delta and action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Regression summary table with coefficient, SE, p-value for MW_delta.\n2. Residual vs fitted and Q–Q diagnostic plots.\n3. Partial correlation coefficient and 95% CI."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The strength of association between SA_delta and target_delta varies significantly across different tasks in successful edits, with some tasks showing stronger coupling.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compute and compare the Pearson correlation between SA_delta and target_delta for each task_id in train.parquet and test for differences across tasks.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet, drop rows with null SA_delta or target_delta.\n2. For each task_id, compute Pearson r and bootstrap (1,000 resamples) its 95% CI.\n3. Create a table of task_id, r, and CIs, then visualize as a bar chart with error bars or a heatmap of correlations.\n4. Perform a one-way ANOVA or meta-analysis test on Fisher z-transformed correlations to assess whether correlations differ significantly among tasks.\n5. Highlight tasks with significantly higher or lower correlation than the overall mean.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of Pearson r and 95% CIs per task_id.\n2. Bar chart or heatmap of SA_delta vs target_delta correlations across tasks.\n3. ANOVA or meta-analytic test statistic and p-value comparing correlations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Successful insert operations involve smaller MW_delta than unsuccessful insert operations.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compare MW_delta distributions between insert operations in train.parquet and train_neg.parquet and test for significant differences.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both datasets, filter to action_type='insert', and drop rows with null MW_delta.\n2. Label records as success (from train) or failure (from train_neg).\n3. Compute group-wise mean, SD, and bootstrap (1,000 resamples) 95% CIs for MW_delta in both groups.\n4. Test for normality in each group and choose either Welch’s t-test or Mann–Whitney U to compare distributions.\n5. Perform the test and record statistic and p-value (adjusted if necessary).\n6. Visualize side-by-side boxplots or violin plots of MW_delta for successful vs unsuccessful insert operations.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table of mean, SD, and 95% CIs of MW_delta for successful vs unsuccessful inserts.\n2. Normality test results.\n3. Test statistic and p-value for the group comparison.\n4. Boxplot or violin plot of MW_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Replace operations in successful edits involve smaller fragment sizes (heavy‐atom count) than replace operations in unsuccessful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Derive fragment_size from the edit SMARTS/SMILES for replace operations and compare between success and failure sets.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet, filter to action_type='replace', drop null edit.\n2. Parse the 'edit' field to extract the fragment SMILES/SMARTS being replaced, as in prior fragment_size extraction.\n3. Use RDKit to convert each fragment into a molecule and count heavy atoms; store as fragment_size.\n4. Label each record as success or failure and compute group-wise mean, SD, and bootstrap 95% CIs for fragment_size.\n5. Test normality and perform Welch’s t-test or Mann–Whitney U to compare fragment_size between groups.\n6. Visualize overlaid histograms or boxplots of fragment_size by success label.",  
      "deliverables":"1. DataFrame with fragment_size column for replace operations.\n2. Summary table of mean, SD, and 95% CIs of fragment_size for successful vs unsuccessful replaces.\n3. Group comparison test statistic and p-value.\n4. Histogram or boxplot comparing fragment_size distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tasks with higher mean target_delta among successful replace edits have higher replace operation success rates across tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Compute per-task replace success rates and test correlation with mean target_delta in successful replace edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load both train.parquet and train_neg.parquet, filter to action_type='replace', drop null target_delta.\n2. For each task_id, count successful replaces (from train) and failed replaces (from train_neg) to compute replace_success_rate = successes/(successes + failures).\n3. For each task_id, compute mean successful replace target_delta.\n4. Across tasks, compute Pearson and Spearman correlation between replace_success_rate and mean target_delta; bootstrap correlations (1,000 resamples) for 95% CIs.\n5. Test significance of correlations and plot a scatter plot of success rate vs mean target_delta with fitted regression line.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table per task_id: replace_success_rate and mean successful replace target_delta.\n2. Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients with 95% CIs and p-values.\n3. Scatter plot of replace_success_rate vs mean target_delta with regression line."  
    }  
  }  
]}
